  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe introduce a fundamentally different approach: a Pareto optimization strategy over sequence points combined with gradient-based refinement. Instead of treating the entire sequence uniformly, we discretize the search space by decomposing the problem into optimizing each sequence index individually while maintaining the global constraint. This allows us to explore extremely diverse configurations by allocating computation time to evaluate different sequence patterns. We view the step function optimization as a multi-objective problem: minimize the maximum autocorrelation while maintaining a sufficiently large sum.\n\nMathematical specification:\nGiven a sequence a of length n, we define the objective J(a) = 2*n*max_i[i](a*convolve(a)[i]) / (sum_j a_j)^2. We develop a three-step mathematical framework:\n1. Fourier-parameterized population structure preservation\n2. Convex combination optimization in the index space\n3. Gradient polishing with nuclear-norm constraints\n\nImplementation procedure:\nOur plan consists of four main stages:\n1. Initialization: Start with a normalized sequence from the given parent (if available)\n2.Parameterization: Map the sequence to a Fourier representation with n_harm harmonics\n3.Optimization: Perform Pareto optimization over the sequence coordinates\n4.Polishing: Apply gradient-based refinement to improve the local minimum\n\nWe implement helper functions for:\n-Fourier parameterization: converting between sequence and Fourier spaces\n-Gradient computation: calculates analytic derivatives of the convolution\n-Feasibility checks: ensures positivity and sum constraints (sum_a \u2265 0.01)\n\nOur optimization uses:\n- Linear resampling to integer sequences\n- Shift operations controlled by perturb_type\n- Dynamic scaling based on sum constraints\n- Nuclear-norm constraints to maintain diversity\n\nThe core search uses the improved_score_surrogate function, which balances:\n- Local gradient steps (using scipy.optimize.minimize for micro optimization)\n-Global diversity preservation (via Fourier-space random walk)\n-Scalable execution across multiple sequence lengths\n\nSearch choices and useful variations are built in:\n- Fixed-length perturbations (shift, taper)\n-Profile-driven length adjustments (factor-of-2 growth)\n-Parametric controls for sharpness/beta (0.5-0.9)\n-Explicit constraints for sum/autocorrelation\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\nThe timer cutoffs are adjusted to account for:\n1.FFT convolution costs (\u223cO(n log n))\n2.Grad descent at each point (\u223cO(10*len))\n3.Fourier updates (\u223c4 + log2(len))\nThe algorithm terminates candidates that exceed resource thresholds.\n\nValidation andreturn contract:\n-Every candidate is re-evaluated with np.inf fallback\n-Evaluations use scipy.optimize.minimize until stabilization\n-If scavenged values are invalid, skip\n-Conservative time estimates: 100s evaluations per length\n-Maximum length support: 1024 (with factor-of-2 expansion possible)\n\nThe function will perform an initial convolution check (step0) and then use Fourier-parameterized gradient descent (main optimization) to minimize J iteratively until deadline or stabilization.\n\nFollows:\n1.Sequential coordinate selection (section 0)\n2.Fourier-parameterized convex combination (section 1)\n3.Dynamic time banking (section 2)\n4.Error handling for invalid coordinates (section 3)\n5.Complete fallback for unfeasible candidates (section 4)\n\nHere's the implementation plan.",
